---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["shift-differential-pay"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    A <strong>shift differential</strong> is extra pay for working a less
    popular shift, such as evenings, nights, weekends or rotating schedules.
    It's usually a fixed amount per hour (for example +$2.00) or a percentage
    of the base rate (for example +10%). No US federal law requires it, but
    when it's paid, federal law <strong>does</strong> require it to be included
    in your regular rate, so it raises your overtime pay as well.
  </p>

  <h2>Common types of differential</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th><th>On a $20.00 base</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Flat amount per hour</td><td>+$2.00 for hours after 6:00 PM</td><td>$22.00 an hour</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Percentage of base rate</td><td>+10% on night shifts</td><td>$22.00 an hour</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Flat amount per shift</td><td>+$25 per overnight shift</td><td>$25 extra for that shift</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weekend or holiday premium</td><td>+$3.00 on Saturday and Sunday</td><td>$23.00 an hour</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Which hours qualify depends on the employer's policy or union contract.
    Some pay the differential for every hour of a qualifying shift; others
    only for the hours that fall inside a set window, such as 11:00 PM to
    7:00 AM. Check the wording, as it changes the number of hours you get it
    for.
  </p>

  <h2>Calculating the pay</h2>
  <p>
    <code>differential pay = qualifying hours &times; differential per hour</code><br />
    <code>percentage differential per hour = base rate &times; percentage</code>
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Example.</strong> A night-shift worker earns $20.00 an hour plus a
    $2.00 differential for 24 qualifying hours a week: 24 &times; $2.00 =
    $48.00 a week. Over a year that's 24 &times; $2.00 &times; 52 =
    <strong>$2,496</strong> on top of base pay, which is worth knowing when
    you compare a night job with a day job.
  </p>

  <h2>Differentials and overtime</h2>
  <p>
    This is where pay stubs often go wrong. Under the Fair Labor Standards
    Act, the regular rate used for overtime must include "such extra premiums
    as nightshift differentials (whether they take the form of a percent of
    the base rate or an addition of so many cents per hour)" (29 CFR
    §778.207(b)). So in any week with overtime, the overtime rate isn't just
    1.5 &times; your base rate.
  </p>
  <p>Use the same method as for <a href="/guides/overtime-with-two-pay-rates/">overtime with two pay rates</a>:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Add all straight-time pay for the week: base pay for every hour, plus all differential pay.</li>
    <li>Divide by total hours worked to get the regular rate.</li>
    <li>Pay half the regular rate on top for each overtime hour.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>
    The worker above puts in 46 hours one week, 24 of them qualifying for the
    $2.00 night differential:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Straight-time pay: 46 &times; $20.00 + 24 &times; $2.00 = $920.00 + $48.00 = <strong>$968.00</strong></li>
    <li>Regular rate: $968.00 &divide; 46 = <strong>$21.0435</strong> an hour</li>
    <li>Overtime premium: 6 &times; 0.5 &times; $21.0435 = <strong>$63.13</strong></li>
    <li>Gross pay: $968.00 + $63.13 = <strong>$1,031.13</strong></li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If the employer leaves the differential out of the overtime rate, the
    premium is 6 &times; 0.5 &times; $20.00 = $60.00 and gross pay is
    $1,028.00, which is <strong>$3.13 short</strong>. That's a small amount
    for one week, but it adds up over a year of regular overtime.
  </p>
  <p>
    The overtime premium in this method is based on the
    <em>average</em> rate for the week, not on whether the overtime hours
    themselves were night or day hours.
  </p>

  <h2>What the law requires, by country</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>United States:</strong> no federal requirement to pay a differential; if one is paid, it goes into the regular rate. Some states and many union contracts have their own rules, especially in healthcare and public-sector work.</li>
    <li><strong>Canada (Ontario):</strong> no general shift-premium requirement in the Employment Standards Act; differentials come from contracts and collective agreements.</li>
    <li><strong>United Kingdom:</strong> no legal requirement to pay more for nights or weekends; any premium is contractual. Regular premiums can count toward holiday pay (see <a href="/guides/uk-overtime-pay-rules/">UK overtime pay rules</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Australia:</strong> modern awards commonly set penalty rates for evenings, nights, weekends and public holidays. Check the award that covers your job.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Is a differential taxed differently?</h2>
  <p>
    No. In the US, differential pay is ordinary wages, subject to the same
    income tax withholding and FICA as base pay. A bigger paycheck can look
    over-withheld, for the same reason overtime does; see
    <a href="/guides/is-overtime-taxed-more/">is overtime taxed more?</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Calculate it</h2>
  <p>
    Work out your regular rate with the steps above, then enter it in the
    <a href="/calculators/overtime-calculator/">Overtime Calculator</a> to split
    regular and overtime pay. The
    <a href="/calculators/hourly-pay-calculator/">Hourly Pay Calculator</a>
    shows what a differential is worth per month and per year, and the
    <a href="/calculators/paycheck-calculator/">Paycheck Calculator</a>
    estimates the take-home.
  </p>

